(Struggling)

1303
00:50:04,118 --> 00:50:07,458
(Shall I wash up now?)

1304
00:50:09,558 --> 00:50:11,788
(Brushing her teeth)

1305
00:50:12,998 --> 00:50:16,568
(She gets done with thoroughly brushing her teeth.)

1306
00:50:18,768 --> 00:50:21,068
(Walking over)

1307
00:50:25,878 --> 00:50:27,578
- What? - She must be heading out.

1308
00:50:30,078 --> 00:50:31,477
(She gets dressed in an all-black outfit.)

1309
00:50:31,478 --> 00:50:32,578
She really loves black.

1310
00:50:34,118 --> 00:50:38,448
It feels like I'm at peace when I wear black.

1311
00:50:39,018 --> 00:50:41,388
All of my skirts and pants are all black.

1312
00:50:41,558 --> 00:50:44,588
Even my tracksuits are all black.

1313
00:50:45,998 --> 00:50:47,357
(Even her glasses are black.)

1314
00:50:47,358 --> 00:50:48,598
Everything is black.

1315
00:50:49,898 --> 00:50:51,298
(Satisfied)

1316
00:50:52,298 --> 00:50:53,868
(Picking up)

1317
00:50:55,168 --> 00:50:58,138
(Shall I head out now?)

1318
00:50:59,538 --> 00:51:03,048
(Tsuki leaves the house after getting dressed in black.)

1319
00:51:05,348 --> 00:51:06,868
It feels a bit like "Hello Franceska."

1320
00:51:09,188 --> 00:51:11,887
(The youngest of the vampire family is heading somewhere?)

1321
00:51:11,888 --> 00:51:14,057
I thought it was a rerun of the MBC show.

1322
00:51:14,058 --> 00:51:15,728
("Hello Franceska")

1323
00:51:16,728 --> 00:51:19,958
(She heads out while showing off her dark energy.)

1324
00:51:21,128 --> 00:51:23,668
(Humming)

1325
00:51:27,898 --> 00:51:29,068
Should I get some carp bread?

1326
00:51:30,738 --> 00:51:32,908
(Exclaiming)

1327
00:51:34,778 --> 00:51:38,148
(She heads in somewhere after overcoming the temptation.)

1328
00:51:38,448 --> 00:51:39,918
Hello.

1329
00:51:42,348 --> 00:51:47,088
(Is she at a supermarket after saying hello naturally?)

1330
00:51:48,658 --> 00:51:52,327
When I bought things like meat online,

1331
00:51:52,328 --> 00:51:55,597
there were times when they weren't as fresh as...

1332
00:51:55,598 --> 00:51:58,128
the ones sold in supermarkets.

1333
00:51:58,368 --> 00:52:00,337
So when it comes to meat or vegetables,

1334
00:52:00,338 --> 00:52:02,638
I tend to go grocery shopping for them.

1335
00:52:03,708 --> 00:52:05,707
Ever since I was young,

1336
00:52:05,708 --> 00:52:09,548
I helped my parents with the cooking.

1337
00:52:09,808 --> 00:52:12,417
I got an oven recently. I bake cookies from time to time.

1338
00:52:12,418 --> 00:52:14,717
- I heard they're hard to make. - She goes through all that work?

1339
00:52:14,718 --> 00:52:16,824
- Check out the kimchi jjigae. - The kimchi jjigae looks amazing.

1340
00:52:16,848 --> 00:52:18,657
Right?

1341
00:52:18,658 --> 00:52:20,518
I make pasta or gambas al ajillo as well.

1342
00:52:20,718 --> 00:52:22,828
I try to make different kinds of dishes.

1343
00:52:23,088 --> 00:52:24,758
You must be a great cook.

1344
00:52:26,658 --> 00:52:29,298
(She heads into the supermarket after picking up a basket.)

1345
00:52:29,668 --> 00:52:30,797
Let's see.

1346
00:52:30,798 --> 00:52:32,468
This was the supermarket look?

1347
00:52:32,638 --> 00:52:33,668
Yes.

1348
00:52:34,098 --> 00:52:37,468
It's just in case I bump into someone.

1349
00:52:37,668 --> 00:52:39,937
"Tsuki isn't that pretty."

1350
00:52:39,938 --> 00:52:42,278
- In case that happens? - I feel hurt when that happens.

1351
00:52:43,508 --> 00:52:47,278
The kimchi is right here.

1352
00:52:47,518 --> 00:52:48,618
Aged kimchi.

1353
00:52:50,518 --> 00:52:51,618
Aged kimchi.

1354
00:52:51,988 --> 00:52:53,318
She went with aged kimchi.

1355
00:52:54,088 --> 00:52:56,957
Pork belly with skin or pork belly.

1356
00:52:56,958 --> 00:52:58,734
(She heads to the meat corner after getting the kimchi.)

1357
00:52:58,758 --> 00:53:01,798
How many grams is this?

1358
00:53:01,958 --> 00:53:04,028
It's 600g.

1359
00:53:04,468 --> 00:53:06,348
- Would this one be better? - She's meticulous.

1360
00:53:07,368 --> 00:53:10,268
What would be good? There are so many of them.

1361
00:53:11,338 --> 00:53:12,877
- Ma'am. - Yes.

1362
00:53:12,878 --> 00:53:14,507
Is there thinly sliced pork belly?

1363
00:53:14,508 --> 00:53:15,648
Yes.

1364
00:53:15,708 --> 00:53:18,147
I want to make braised kimchi and pork belly rolls.

1365
00:53:18,148 --> 00:53:19,817
- Braised kimchi with pork belly. - Braised kimchi with pork belly.

1366
00:53:19,818 --> 00:53:21,417
That needs a lot of work to make.

1367
00:53:21,418 --> 00:53:22,578
There's pork belly with skin.

1368
00:53:22,888 --> 00:53:24,348
Which one would be better?

1369
00:53:26,218 --> 00:53:27,757
- I'll choose one for you. - That's great.

1370
00:53:27,758 --> 00:53:29,458
- The one that looks delicious. - Great!

1371
00:53:30,658 --> 00:53:32,457
I can't tell the difference.

1372
00:53:32,458 --> 00:53:33,997
It's best to ask the ladies at the supermarket.

1373
00:53:33,998 --> 00:53:35,028
Right.

1374
00:53:35,728 --> 00:53:36,767
- Thank you! - Here you are.

1375
00:53:36,768 --> 00:53:38,968
- Right. Thank you. - Thank you.

1376
00:53:41,798 --> 00:53:44,808
Pollack roe. I need to get some pollack roe.

1377
00:53:45,708 --> 00:53:47,008
Is it here?

1378
00:53:48,978 --> 00:53:51,147
- Ma'am, where's the pollack roe? - Yes?

1379
00:53:51,148 --> 00:53:53,018
- Sorry? - Where's the pollack roe?

1380
00:53:53,448 --> 00:53:54,647
- The what? - Pollack roe.

1381
00:53:54,648 --> 00:53:56,348
- Pollack roe? - Gosh!

1382
00:53:56,488 --> 00:53:58,858
- It's really hard to pronounce. - Over there.

1383
00:53:58,918 --> 00:54:00,657
- "Pollack roe." - "Pollack roe."

1384
00:54:00,658 --> 00:54:02,688
Pollack row?

1385
00:54:03,158 --> 00:54:04,188
Roe!

1386
00:54:04,628 --> 00:54:06,597
The pronunciation changes when the two words are together.

1387
00:54:06,598 --> 00:54:07,657
- "Pollack roe." - "Pollack roe."

1388
00:54:07,658 --> 00:54:08,767
"Pollack roe."

1389
00:54:08,768 --> 00:54:10,897
- "Pollack roe." - "Pollack roe."

1390
00:54:10,898 --> 00:54:12,337
People will understand you much better that way.

1391
00:54:12,338 --> 00:54:13,368
Really?

1392
00:54:14,498 --> 00:54:16,667
- Pollack roe. - Right.

1393
00:54:16,668 --> 00:54:18,008
Thank you!

1394
00:54:19,208 --> 00:54:20,707
(Impressed)

1395
00:54:20,708 --> 00:54:21,738
It's big!

1396
00:54:22,978 --> 00:54:24,377
- It's plump. - Gosh. It looks tasty.

1397
00:54:24,378 --> 00:54:26,117
(The pollack roe looks plump.)

1398
00:54:26,118 --> 00:54:27,917
Okay. Thank you.

1399
00:54:27,918 --> 00:54:29,118
(Okay. This is it.)

1400
00:54:30,948 --> 00:54:34,657
If I ask the ladies there, they help me right away.

1401
00:54:34,658 --> 00:54:36,627
It's really convenient.

1402
00:54:36,628 --> 00:54:38,228
Well,

1403
00:54:38,658 --> 00:54:40,227
they're my seniors in life.

1404
00:54:40,228 --> 00:54:43,268
- I can fully trust them. - Seniors in life.

1405
00:54:44,328 --> 00:54:45,697
(She looks around after picking up the pollack roe.)

1406
00:54:45,698 --> 00:54:46,998
What is that?

1407
00:54:48,038 --> 00:54:49,038
(Sniffing)

1408
00:54:49,039 --> 00:54:50,508
Gosh.

1409
00:54:52,408 --> 00:54:53,737
- Gosh! - Try some.

1410
00:54:53,738 --> 00:54:54,777
- Really? - Yes.

1411
00:54:54,778 --> 00:54:56,247
- It's the tasting booth. - Thank you!

1412
00:54:56,248 --> 00:54:57,808
I love Korean culture.

1413
00:54:58,208 --> 00:54:59,277
Do they not have tasting booths in Japan?

1414
00:54:59,278 --> 00:55:00,348
They rarely have them.

1415
00:55:00,848 --> 00:55:01,917
I got so surprised.

1416
00:55:01,918 --> 00:55:03,247
If you go to a supermarket in Korea,

1417
00:55:03,248 --> 00:55:05,147
there are tasting booths with each step you take.

1418
00:55:05,148 --> 00:55:06,217
After a round...

1419
00:55:06,218 --> 00:55:07,587
- That's true. - Right.

1420
00:55:07,588 --> 00:55:09,087
You feel full after going through all of them.

1421
00:55:09,088 --> 00:55:10,588
Right. You feel full after a round.

1422
00:55:11,128 --> 00:55:12,497
- It's hot. Be careful. - Thank you.

1423
00:55:12,498 --> 00:55:13,498
Right!

1424
00:55:15,158 --> 00:55:17,868
It's being sold at a great price today.

1425
00:55:18,198 --> 00:55:19,437
(It's a taste that makes her eyes roll around.)

1426
00:55:19,438 --> 00:55:21,138
- It's good, isn't it? - Yes. It's good.

1427
00:55:21,738 --> 00:55:23,007
- Can I get one? - Would you like one?

1428
00:55:23,008 --> 00:55:24,237
(Can I get one?)

1429
00:55:24,238 --> 00:55:25,308
One?

1430
00:55:26,078 --> 00:55:27,577
- Thank you. - Thank you.

1431
00:55:27,578 --> 00:55:28,898
- Thank you. - Hope you enjoy it.

1432
00:55:29,948 --> 00:55:31,708
Fascinatingly enough, it tastes good there.

1433
00:55:32,008 --> 00:55:33,747
But it doesn't taste the same at home.

1434
00:55:33,748 --> 00:55:35,448
- You should eat a little. - Right.

1435
00:55:36,218 --> 00:55:38,648
Here it is.

1436
00:55:39,318 --> 00:55:41,058
Okay. For Korean cuisine.

1437
00:55:42,858 --> 00:55:44,327
- Hello. - Hello.

1438
00:55:44,328 --> 00:55:45,527
(She hurries over to the counter.)

1439
00:55:45,528 --> 00:55:46,558
Thank you.

1440
00:55:48,498 --> 00:55:50,668
Here we go.

1441
00:55:51,598 --> 00:55:52,667
(She meticulously puts the things into the grocery bag she brought.)

1442
00:55:52,668 --> 00:55:54,197
- She brought a grocery bag. - She carries that around.

1443
00:55:54,198 --> 00:55:56,238
She has a pretty one.

1444
00:55:57,108 --> 00:55:58,567
- Thank you. - Goodbye.

1445
00:55:58,568 --> 00:55:59,908
Bye!

1446
00:56:00,678 --> 00:56:01,708
Okay.

1447
00:56:02,138 --> 00:56:05,547
(She returns home.)

1448
00:56:05,548 --> 00:56:06,778
Gosh.

1449
00:56:07,918 --> 00:56:09,018
(Straining)

1450
00:56:11,448 --> 00:56:12,988
There we go.

1451
00:56:14,658 --> 00:56:17,228
I should try making it without looking at the recipe.

1452
00:56:19,698 --> 00:56:21,997
(She gets the condiments that she will be using.)

1453
00:56:21,998 --> 00:56:24,228
Soy sauce and cooking wine.

1454
00:56:25,938 --> 00:56:27,698
(I should wash my hands thoroughly as well.)

1455
00:56:28,468 --> 00:56:29,537
Chili powder.

1456
00:56:29,538 --> 00:56:31,138
(She starts cooking in earnest.)

1457
00:56:31,838 --> 00:56:34,178
(She pours out the chili powder without measuring it.)

1458
00:56:34,878 --> 00:56:37,077
I'll add some cooking wine...

1459
00:56:37,078 --> 00:56:38,708
and there's the sauce.

1460
00:56:39,948 --> 00:56:42,077
- A little bit of pepper. - Oh, good.

1461
00:56:42,078 --> 00:56:45,018
She seems to know what she's doing with the sauce.

1462
00:56:45,188 --> 00:56:49,157
- My father was a chef in the past. - Gosh.

1463
00:56:49,158 --> 00:56:51,428
- She must have the cooking genes. - Right.

1464
00:56:52,398 --> 00:56:53,528
Okay.

1465
00:56:54,728 --> 00:56:55,767
Roll them up.

1466
00:56:55,768 --> 00:56:57,728
(She neatly spreads out a slice of kimchi first.)

1467
00:56:58,768 --> 00:56:59,997
(She cuts the meat with scissors.)

1468
00:56:59,998 --> 00:57:02,737
- I learned that in Korea. - This is so nice.

1469
00:57:02,738 --> 00:57:04,408
Cutting meat with scissors.

1470
00:57:04,838 --> 00:57:06,107
(Finding her to be cute)

1471
00:57:06,108 --> 00:57:09,007
The strangest thing for foreigners who visit restaurants in Korea...

1472
00:57:09,008 --> 00:57:11,907
for the first time is the scissors on the table.

1473
00:57:11,908 --> 00:57:13,077
- Right. - Really?

1474
00:57:13,078 --> 00:57:14,294
They don't understand why the scissors are on the table.

1475
00:57:14,318 --> 00:57:15,998
- Others don't use scissors? - They don't.

1476
00:57:16,118 --> 00:57:17,847
Scissors are used only for cutting paper.

1477
00:57:17,848 --> 00:57:20,129
They don't understand why they are used for cutting food.

1478
00:57:20,358 --> 00:57:21,487
They get shocked by it.

1479
00:57:21,488 --> 00:57:23,488
But as K-food spread out,

1480
00:57:23,988 --> 00:57:25,327
scissors were so convenient with food.

1481
00:57:25,328 --> 00:57:26,527
- Right. - Right.

1482
00:57:26,528 --> 00:57:27,528
It's the best.

1483
00:57:27,529 --> 00:57:28,927
- Knives don't work well. - No.

1484
00:57:28,928 --> 00:57:30,727
You have to go back and forth so many times.

1485
00:57:30,728 --> 00:57:32,227
That is why many people started using scissors.

1486
00:57:32,228 --> 00:57:33,238
Right.

1487
00:57:37,308 --> 00:57:39,378
Yes.

1488
00:57:40,538 --> 00:57:41,877
(Rolling)

1489
00:57:41,878 --> 00:57:43,148
There we go.

1490
00:57:43,648 --> 00:57:44,808
- It looks delicious. - Okay.

1491
00:57:45,278 --> 00:57:46,948
Okay. There we go.

1492
00:57:47,918 --> 00:57:49,587
- She's so talented. - Right.

1493
00:57:49,588 --> 00:57:51,587
It's like mille-feuille nabe.

1494
00:57:51,588 --> 00:57:52,818
The "mille-feuille nabe?"

1495
00:57:55,058 --> 00:57:57,658
(The mille-feuille nabe-like braised kimchi is put on the stove.)

1496
00:57:57,758 --> 00:57:58,758
Here we go.

1497
00:57:59,458 --> 00:58:01,128
Okay. This looks great.

1498
00:58:03,168 --> 00:58:05,028
(Pouring into the bowl of sauce)

1499
00:58:05,328 --> 00:58:07,467
Some broth will be poured on top.

1500
00:58:07,468 --> 00:58:09,538
There we go. This is great.

1501
00:58:12,978 --> 00:58:14,637
(A generous amount of allulose for its sweetness)

1502
00:58:14,638 --> 00:58:16,478
- Allulose. - For its sweetness.

1503
00:58:16,748 --> 00:58:18,207
Okay.

1504
00:58:18,208 --> 00:58:19,478
- It looks good. - There we go.

1505
00:58:19,578 --> 00:58:20,617
Right. It looks delicious.

1506
00:58:20,618 --> 00:58:21,648
Right.

1507
00:58:21,848 --> 00:58:23,018
It's getting warmer.

1508
00:58:23,388 --> 00:58:25,468
I want to try this at home as it looks easy to make.

1509
00:58:27,058 --> 00:58:28,757
(While the braised kimchi boils...)

1510
00:58:28,758 --> 00:58:30,288
(Thinking)

1511
00:58:31,558 --> 00:58:32,858
It's too bright.

1512
00:58:33,158 --> 00:58:34,298
Should I give it a try?

1513
00:58:36,398 --> 00:58:39,398
(She gets something out of the drawer while humming.)

1514
00:58:42,708 --> 00:58:47,078
(She also gets something from underneath the bed.)

1515
00:58:48,608 --> 00:58:51,278
I can do it. I really can.

1516
00:58:51,548 --> 00:58:54,117
- I believe in myself. - What is that?

1517
00:58:54,118 --> 00:58:55,748
- What is she doing? - What is that?

1518
00:58:56,548 --> 00:58:59,357
As it's my day off,

1519
00:58:59,358 --> 00:59:01,857
I wanted to take care of my house chores.

1520
00:59:01,858 --> 00:59:04,027
I wanted to install the curtains.

1521
00:59:04,028 --> 00:59:06,198
That was why I got everything out.

1522
00:59:06,658 --> 00:59:08,257
Since I don't like the sunlight,

1523
00:59:08,258 --> 00:59:09,827
I wanted to make it as dark as possible.

1524
00:59:09,828 --> 00:59:11,398
- It's another layer. - Another layer?

1525
00:59:12,938 --> 00:59:15,018
(She gets something else out from the utility room.)

1526
00:59:16,538 --> 00:59:19,137
(Tsuki gets out a ladder to install the curtains.)

1527
00:59:19,138 --> 00:59:20,277
She has a ladder at home?

1528
00:59:20,278 --> 00:59:22,477
- She has a ladder at home. - I have everything.

1529
00:59:22,478 --> 00:59:23,777
I can do this.

1530
00:59:23,778 --> 00:59:25,247
(Fascinated)

1531
00:59:25,248 --> 00:59:26,417
Let's go.

1532
00:59:26,418 --> 00:59:28,918
(Climbing up)

1533
00:59:30,318 --> 00:59:32,148
It won't be easy.

1534
00:59:33,258 --> 00:59:35,927
I want to take care of everything on my own if I can.

1535
00:59:35,928 --> 00:59:38,088
I will accept it if I can't do it...

1536
00:59:38,188 --> 00:59:39,357
and call for help.

1537
00:59:39,358 --> 00:59:42,057
I think I'm someone who tries to get things done on my own...

1538
00:59:42,058 --> 00:59:43,528
until the very end.

1539
00:59:44,398 --> 00:59:47,038
I should give it a try first.

1540
00:59:48,038 --> 00:59:53,338
(She uses her bare hands without any tools.)

1541
00:59:53,778 --> 00:59:55,537
- It's slippery. - Use a screwdriver.

1542
00:59:55,538 --> 00:59:57,047
She's using her hand to drill?

1543
00:59:57,048 --> 00:59:59,647
- You're drilling it like this? - You're using your hand?

1544
00:59:59,648 --> 01:00:00,977
Some use their nails to drill it.

1545
01:00:00,978 --> 01:00:02,377
- Oh, like this? - You know, right?

1546
01:00:02,378 --> 01:00:04,618
- Totally. - And their skin would get scraped.

1547
01:00:05,148 --> 01:00:07,388
- Right. - That's right.

1548
01:00:10,188 --> 01:00:13,098
(Struggling to drive a nail with her hand)

1549
01:00:14,758 --> 01:00:16,628
(Loose)

1550
01:00:18,298 --> 01:00:21,097
(Oh, right. I forgot the braised kimchi.)

1551
01:00:21,098 --> 01:00:22,197
The braised kimchi.

1552
01:00:22,198 --> 01:00:23,598
(The braised kimchi is boiling up.)

1553
01:00:24,138 --> 01:00:26,338
(I should go check on the braised kimchi.)

1554
01:00:26,508 --> 01:00:28,838
(Checking on the braised kimchi, leaving the curtains)

1555
01:00:29,708 --> 01:00:30,907
It must've turned out well.

1556
01:00:30,908 --> 01:00:32,177
- Right? - It looks delicious.

1557
01:00:32,178 --> 01:00:33,448
- Right? - It looks good.

1558
01:00:35,748 --> 01:00:37,587
- It looks really good. - It looks good.

1559
01:00:37,588 --> 01:00:38,618
Yes.

1560
01:00:46,828 --> 01:00:47,997
- It was sweet, right? - Maybe it was too good?

1561
01:00:47,998 --> 01:00:48,998
(Carefully tasting it again)

1562
01:00:48,999 --> 01:00:50,119
It looks like it's too good.

1563
01:00:50,668 --> 01:00:51,768
- What? - I was wrong.

1564
01:00:52,328 --> 01:00:53,798
Gosh, what do I do?

1565
01:00:55,738 --> 01:00:58,467
It tasted too artificial.

1566
01:00:58,468 --> 01:01:00,438
- Why? - How can it taste bad with those?

1567
01:01:01,738 --> 01:01:03,308
(Serious)

1568
01:01:03,708 --> 01:01:05,178
Is it because of allulose?

1569
01:01:06,518 --> 01:01:07,618
What is the problem?

1570
01:01:09,248 --> 01:01:10,948
Hold on.

1571
01:01:11,848 --> 01:01:13,657
(The house is a mess, and her cooking isn't going well.)

1572
01:01:13,658 --> 01:01:16,228
I'm making braised kimchi,

1573
01:01:17,028 --> 01:01:18,888
and I added allulose.

1574
01:01:19,258 --> 01:01:20,928
But it tastes really weird.

1575
01:01:21,098 --> 01:01:23,428
What went wrong with it?

1576
01:01:24,728 --> 01:01:26,998
"This happens often." What?

1577
01:01:27,138 --> 01:01:28,668
"Happens often?"

1578
01:01:28,898 --> 01:01:31,037
"It must be because of these reasons."

1579
01:01:31,038 --> 01:01:33,578
"The unique aftertaste of allulose."

1580
01:01:34,178 --> 01:01:36,078
Exactly! The unique aftertaste.

1581
01:01:36,548 --> 01:01:37,608
Seriously!

1582
01:01:38,048 --> 01:01:41,048
The aftertaste is killing me! Darn.

1583
01:01:42,118 --> 01:01:43,478
That was the best Korean you used.

1584
01:01:44,148 --> 01:01:46,058
- Out of what I saw. - I know.

1585
01:01:47,358 --> 01:01:49,528
Gosh! I should've just added sugar.

1586
01:01:49,688 --> 01:01:51,087
- I shouldn't have bought this. - It's for syrup.

1587
01:01:51,088 --> 01:01:53,797
- That's for dessert. - You added allulose for dessert.

1588
01:01:53,798 --> 01:01:55,058
Oh, it's different?

1589
01:01:55,728 --> 01:01:58,628
"Add a bit of sugar, plum extract, or oligosaccharide."

1590
01:01:59,028 --> 01:02:01,498
"Add more doenjang or gochujang."

1591
01:02:02,098 --> 01:02:03,568
"Add sesame oil or perilla oil."

1592
01:02:03,868 --> 01:02:05,978
I should add everything. Hold on.

1593
01:02:06,208 --> 01:02:07,928
- Then, I'll add sesame oil. - Gosh, don't.

1594
01:02:08,078 --> 01:02:09,558
- You'll ruin it. - I have gochujang.

1595
01:02:10,008 --> 01:02:12,478
I have Cheongyang red peppers. Is it Cheongyang red pepper?

1596
01:02:13,018 --> 01:02:14,417
- It's Cheongyang red pepper. - She has...

1597
01:02:14,418 --> 01:02:15,847
- Did they go bad? - basic ingredients at home.

1598
01:02:15,848 --> 01:02:16,848
That's right!

1599
01:02:16,849 --> 01:02:19,188
Should I add sugar? But it's past its expiration date.

1600
01:02:19,388 --> 01:02:21,658
I should just add it.

1601
01:02:21,918 --> 01:02:23,028
Let's just add it.

1602
01:02:23,358 --> 01:02:25,257
It's okay. You won't die.

1603
01:02:25,258 --> 01:02:26,458
Will sugar kill you?

1604
01:02:26,628 --> 01:02:27,758
No, it won't.

1605
01:02:28,228 --> 01:02:30,328
You have a problem? No, I don't.

1606
01:02:30,968 --> 01:02:32,368
Just give it a try.

1607
01:02:34,968 --> 01:02:38,308
(Scraping all the gochujang)

1608
01:02:39,108 --> 01:02:41,107
- All right. - She finished it so easily.

1609
01:02:41,108 --> 01:02:42,177
- I know. - Right?

1610
01:02:42,178 --> 01:02:44,738
- It'd have taken me 2 hours. - It'd take me 2 and a half hours.

1611
01:02:44,808 --> 01:02:45,808
(Deep breaths)

1612
01:02:45,809 --> 01:02:46,818
Please...

1613
01:02:47,348 --> 01:02:49,917
(Gosh, please...)

1614
01:02:49,918 --> 01:02:51,488
(Expectant)

1615
01:02:51,888 --> 01:02:54,088
(With a desperate heart...)

1616
01:02:58,158 --> 01:03:00,028
(Crying)

1617
01:03:00,358 --> 01:03:01,528
(What do we do?)

1618
01:03:02,668 --> 01:03:04,427
(Dashing out of shock)

1619
01:03:04,428 --> 01:03:05,567
(Clattering)

1620
01:03:05,568 --> 01:03:06,798
(Laughing)

1621
01:03:07,607 --> 01:03:08,646
Please...

1622
01:03:08,647 --> 01:03:10,687
(Tsuki revived the braised kimchi that was ruined.)

1623
01:03:14,217 --> 01:03:15,987
(Crying)

1624
01:03:16,387 --> 01:03:18,447
(What do we do?)

1625
01:03:18,757 --> 01:03:20,556
(Dashing out of shock)

1626
01:03:20,557 --> 01:03:21,656
(Clattering)

1627
01:03:21,657 --> 01:03:22,727
(Laughing)

1628
01:03:25,927 --> 01:03:28,996
(Crying)

1629
01:03:28,997 --> 01:03:31,043
I want to try it. How does it taste that she's crying?

1630
01:03:31,067 --> 01:03:32,166
I want to try it too.

1631
01:03:32,167 --> 01:03:33,896
- I'm really curious. - I'm really curious.

1632
01:03:33,897 --> 01:03:35,967
- I want to taste it. - It makes me more curious.

1633
01:03:36,167 --> 01:03:39,407
It was the worst taste I'd ever had.

1634
01:03:39,637 --> 01:03:41,137
Then why did you say...

1635
01:03:41,537 --> 01:03:44,776
- that you have a knack for cooking? - I'm so embarrassed.

1636
01:03:44,777 --> 01:03:45,976
Oh, gosh. No!

1637
01:03:45,977 --> 01:03:47,947
Dad! Help me.

1638
01:03:49,847 --> 01:03:52,987
I didn't know food could be this inedible.

1639
01:03:53,387 --> 01:03:55,257
Seriously. Let me think.

1640
01:03:56,157 --> 01:03:57,827
I don't know what to do.

1641
01:03:59,427 --> 01:04:01,197
(Crying)

1642
01:04:06,367 --> 01:04:07,397
Are you crying?

1643
01:04:07,537 --> 01:04:08,567
Why are you crying?

1644
01:04:09,167 --> 01:04:10,206
Did you get a reality check?

1645
01:04:10,207 --> 01:04:12,206
I got a reality check. I was annoyed.

1646
01:04:12,207 --> 01:04:14,106
- Oh, you were annoyed. - So, you don't just cry...

1647
01:04:14,107 --> 01:04:15,136
when you're running.

1648
01:04:15,137 --> 01:04:16,747
I feel like crying.

1649
01:04:16,977 --> 01:04:19,546
(She bawls in front of bad food too.)

1650
01:04:19,547 --> 01:04:21,177
Of course. She gave it so much effort.

1651
01:04:21,817 --> 01:04:23,447
Oh, my. She's bawling.

1652
01:04:23,587 --> 01:04:24,886
It's so bad.

1653
01:04:24,887 --> 01:04:26,363
(She's overwhelmed because it's so bad.)

1654
01:04:26,387 --> 01:04:29,126
- I didn't know what to do. - Hey, you... It's like...

1655
01:04:29,127 --> 01:04:30,127
(What do I do now?)

1656
01:04:30,727 --> 01:04:31,786
I think you're the worst cook out of the members...

1657
01:04:31,787 --> 01:04:32,857
who visited us recently.

1658
01:04:34,257 --> 01:04:35,426
No, don't be prejudiced.

1659
01:04:35,427 --> 01:04:37,227
People will get the wrong idea. This is bad.

1660
01:04:37,297 --> 01:04:38,497
I'm good at cooking!

1661
01:04:40,467 --> 01:04:43,167
When it tastes bad,

1662
01:04:43,667 --> 01:04:47,637
it becomes a big better if you add lots of scallions.

1663
01:04:49,177 --> 01:04:50,883
- He's being considerate. - Oh, lots of scallions?

1664
01:04:50,907 --> 01:04:51,976
One thing for sure is that...

1665
01:04:51,977 --> 01:04:53,146
it'll solve everything if you add ramyeon base.

1666
01:04:53,147 --> 01:04:54,523
- That'd be the best. - That's true.

1667
01:04:54,547 --> 01:04:55,616
- I didn't know. - Ramyeon base solves everything.

1668
01:04:55,617 --> 01:04:56,937
- Really? - It solves everything.

1669
01:04:58,547 --> 01:05:00,457
I can do this.

1670
01:05:00,857 --> 01:05:02,557
Add water.

1671
01:05:04,857 --> 01:05:07,956
(Shaking mysterious powder)

1672
01:05:07,957 --> 01:05:08,997
Is it ramyeon base?

1673
01:05:09,327 --> 01:05:10,567
It's Japanese seasoning powder.

1674
01:05:11,027 --> 01:05:12,096
That'd be okay.

1675
01:05:12,097 --> 01:05:13,236
And Korean seasoning powder.

1676
01:05:13,237 --> 01:05:14,913
(Adding lots of Korean seasoning powder as well)

1677
01:05:14,937 --> 01:05:16,166
Please!

1678
01:05:16,167 --> 01:05:17,336
(Adding lots of chili powder as well)

1679
01:05:17,337 --> 01:05:18,877
That'd taste better.

1680
01:05:19,077 --> 01:05:21,637
She's using more sauces than the chefs in "Culinary Class Wars."

1681
01:05:22,077 --> 01:05:24,307
It's okay. Just...

1682
01:05:25,577 --> 01:05:29,017
It's okay even if you fail.

1683
01:05:30,647 --> 01:05:31,686
(Opening)

1684
01:05:31,687 --> 01:05:32,757
Let's see.

1685
01:05:33,757 --> 01:05:36,327
- It looks amazing, right? - I think it'll be delicious.

1686
01:05:36,727 --> 01:05:40,127
All right.

1687
01:05:42,097 --> 01:05:43,197
It's so pretty.

1688
01:05:43,797 --> 01:05:45,997
(Taking something)

1689
01:05:46,137 --> 01:05:47,566
Sprinkling.

1690
01:05:47,567 --> 01:05:49,006
She's even sprinkling furikake on top.

1691
01:05:49,007 --> 01:05:50,667
I wanted to have a proper meal.

1692
01:05:51,407 --> 01:05:53,636
Pollack roe?

1693
01:05:53,637 --> 01:05:56,647
(I can't leave out pollack roe.)

1694
01:05:57,307 --> 01:05:58,407
It's so cute!

1695
01:05:58,607 --> 01:06:00,077
That must be really delicious.

1696
01:06:01,847 --> 01:06:02,847
(Amazed)

1697
01:06:04,847 --> 01:06:08,687
(It's a must to take a photo of it.)

1698
01:06:08,857 --> 01:06:09,956
A photo she'll delete anyway.

1699
01:06:09,957 --> 01:06:13,257
(She'll delete that photo anyway.)

1700
01:06:14,157 --> 01:06:17,327
That's the first thing you'll delete when you run out of space.

1701
01:06:17,497 --> 01:06:19,496
- The landscape and food photos. - You don't even look at them.

1702
01:06:19,497 --> 01:06:20,777
You don't even give it a glance.

1703
01:06:21,237 --> 01:06:22,266
And the photos of sky come next.

1704
01:06:22,267 --> 01:06:23,706
(Sky photos are 2nd in line to be deleted.)

1705
01:06:23,707 --> 01:06:25,436
- Sky photos! - Sky photos come next.

1706
01:06:25,437 --> 01:06:27,183
- Then I delete my selfies too. - That's right.

1707
01:06:27,207 --> 01:06:29,006
In the end, you're left with photos of your dogs and cats only.

1708
01:06:29,007 --> 01:06:30,047
That's true.

1709
01:06:31,347 --> 01:06:34,547
(I did such a good job!)

1710
01:06:35,617 --> 01:06:36,946
(Braised kimchi with all sorts of sauces is done!)

1711
01:06:36,947 --> 01:06:38,387
You plated them nicely.

1712
01:06:39,117 --> 01:06:40,187
It's perfect.

1713
01:06:41,017 --> 01:06:42,533
How long did it take you to make that?

1714
01:06:42,557 --> 01:06:43,987
Around two and a half hours?

1715
01:06:44,187 --> 01:06:45,696
That's like us.

1716
01:06:45,697 --> 01:06:47,027
Two and a half hours?

1717
01:06:47,127 --> 01:06:49,327
It seems like you made kimchi during that time as well.

1718
01:06:50,667 --> 01:06:51,796
You could go to Japan...

1719
01:06:51,797 --> 01:06:53,317
- with that amount of time. - Totally.

1720
01:06:53,497 --> 01:06:55,236
I could go back and forth.

1721
01:06:55,237 --> 01:06:56,506
- You could go to Fukuoka. - It's possible.

1722
01:06:56,507 --> 01:06:57,947
You could go and see your dad, even.

1723
01:06:58,507 --> 01:07:00,547
It's totally possible to go to Japan and come back.

1724
01:07:01,037 --> 01:07:02,307
Thank you for the food.

1725
01:07:03,777 --> 01:07:06,058
(The first meal she made for herself after ups and downs)

1726
01:07:07,377 --> 01:07:08,446
Thank you for the food.

1727
01:07:08,447 --> 01:07:09,616
(Trying the kimchi first)

1728
01:07:09,617 --> 01:07:12,457
(Crunchy)

1729
01:07:13,187 --> 01:07:14,216
(Then taking a big bite of meat)

1730
01:07:14,217 --> 01:07:15,317
It must be delicious.

1731
01:07:16,287 --> 01:07:17,427
It must be fine.

1732
01:07:18,227 --> 01:07:20,396
(Exclaiming)

1733
01:07:20,397 --> 01:07:22,497
- How was it? - It was good.

1734
01:07:23,267 --> 01:07:25,637
(She can't stop eating.)

1735
01:07:26,367 --> 01:07:30,007
(It's so good. I'm exhilarated.)

1736
01:07:31,107 --> 01:07:36,077
(Taking a bite with a big piece of pollack roe)

1737
01:07:36,777 --> 01:07:40,247
(Eyes going wide)

1738
01:07:41,047 --> 01:07:46,287
(Yes, this is it.)

1739
01:07:47,187 --> 01:07:50,357
(Happy)

1740
01:07:51,127 --> 01:07:54,097
(Applauding the braised kimchi I made)

1741
01:07:54,527 --> 01:07:56,126
It's delicious.

1742
01:07:56,127 --> 01:07:57,166
(She finished the whole braised kimchi.)

1743
01:07:57,167 --> 01:07:59,497
She ate them all.

1744
01:07:59,967 --> 01:08:03,407
(Getting up)

1745
01:08:04,007 --> 01:08:08,607
(She brushes her teeth right away.)

1746
01:08:09,447 --> 01:08:11,706
(Tsuki puts on a coat.)

1747
01:08:11,707 --> 01:08:12,817
Where are you going again?

1748
01:08:14,017 --> 01:08:15,346
You haven't installed the curtains yet.

1749
01:08:15,347 --> 01:08:16,916
Considering the amount of time she takes to cook,

1750
01:08:16,917 --> 01:08:18,447
she should get new ingredients...

1751
01:08:18,817 --> 01:08:20,386
and start cooking again.

1752
01:08:20,387 --> 01:08:21,616
She needs to get ready for dinner.

1753
01:08:21,617 --> 01:08:23,103
(It looks like she'll prepare dinner.)

1754
01:08:23,127 --> 01:08:24,856
(Shall I go out now?)

1755
01:08:24,857 --> 01:08:26,096
- Here we go. - She's really diligent.

1756
01:08:26,097 --> 01:08:28,296
- I know. She's really diligent. - She doesn't stay seated.

1757
01:08:28,297 --> 01:08:29,927
She must be tired by now.

1758
01:08:30,127 --> 01:08:32,007
She could lie down on the couch after her meal.

1759
01:08:32,867 --> 01:08:34,297
Hello.

1760
01:08:34,497 --> 01:08:36,037
- Yes, hello. - Hello.

1761
01:08:37,337 --> 01:08:40,737
(Going somewhere in a taxi)

1762
01:08:41,577 --> 01:08:43,047
The weather is great today.

1763
01:08:44,247 --> 01:08:46,817
(Gazing deeply)

1764
01:08:48,277 --> 01:08:50,387
It's a lot warmer today, right?

1765
01:08:51,717 --> 01:08:54,617
Yes, it's less cold now. It's warm.

1766
01:08:56,127 --> 01:09:00,096
Your pink shirt makes it feel like spring.

1767
01:09:00,097 --> 01:09:01,197
Is that so?

1768
01:09:01,657 --> 01:09:02,667
It's pretty.

1769
01:09:03,927 --> 01:09:06,696
Not many people can pull off pink.

1770
01:09:06,697 --> 01:09:07,797
Gosh, thank you.

1771
01:09:09,937 --> 01:09:12,307
While you drive around,

1772
01:09:12,837 --> 01:09:15,037
do you get a lot of foreign passengers?

1773
01:09:15,507 --> 01:09:16,547
Yes, I do.

1774
01:09:17,647 --> 01:09:19,276
I'm Japanese.

1775
01:09:19,277 --> 01:09:21,447
- You're from Japan? - Yes.

1776
01:09:22,487 --> 01:09:26,087
There were so many things I wanted to do in Korea.

1777
01:09:26,257 --> 01:09:28,287
Like going to Gyeongbokgung...

1778
01:09:28,517 --> 01:09:29,956
dressed in hanbok.

1779
01:09:29,957 --> 01:09:33,357
I wanted to try those basic things, but I haven't got the chance to.

1780
01:09:34,527 --> 01:09:36,296
You couldn't do it even though you've been here for ten years.

1781
01:09:36,297 --> 01:09:38,497
No. I want to.

1782
01:09:39,097 --> 01:09:42,736
You should try experiencing many things. It'll be nice.

1783
01:09:42,737 --> 01:09:43,837
Sure.

1784
01:09:44,267 --> 01:09:45,737
What's your hobby?

1785
01:09:46,507 --> 01:09:48,137
Hobby? It's running marathons.

1786
01:09:48,237 --> 01:09:49,506
- Really? - Yes.

1787
01:09:49,507 --> 01:09:51,877
- This is crazy. - I'm a marathon lover.

1788
01:09:51,947 --> 01:09:54,477
- Have you seen "Extreme84?" - Yes, I have.

1789
01:09:54,717 --> 01:09:58,017
The woman in that show...

1790
01:09:58,247 --> 01:10:00,687
Do you remember the woman who cried while running?

1791
01:10:01,217 --> 01:10:02,526
Oh, dear.

1792
01:10:02,527 --> 01:10:03,656
I think I've seen her.

1793
01:10:03,657 --> 01:10:05,457
- That was me. - Is that so?

1794
01:10:05,757 --> 01:10:07,397
It was me, sir!

1795
01:10:07,727 --> 01:10:09,026
The one who cried while running.

1796
01:10:09,027 --> 01:10:12,467
The one who bawled while running was me.

1797
01:10:12,837 --> 01:10:14,996
Here. Look at me.

1798
01:10:14,997 --> 01:10:16,066
(Do you recognize me?)

1799
01:10:16,067 --> 01:10:17,106
- Right? - You do look like her...

1800
01:10:17,107 --> 01:10:18,107
without the glasses.

1801
01:10:18,108 --> 01:10:19,307
- Right? - Yes.

1802
01:10:19,377 --> 01:10:20,897
(Feeling proud that he recognizes her)

1803
01:10:21,307 --> 01:10:23,577
How fascinating. I can't believe it.

1804
01:10:24,207 --> 01:10:25,546
We got here before we knew it while talking.

1805
01:10:25,547 --> 01:10:28,216
Right. It was too short.

1806
01:10:28,217 --> 01:10:30,377
You should've asked him to make about two more rounds.

1807
01:10:31,287 --> 01:10:32,387
That's hilarious.

1808
01:10:32,717 --> 01:10:35,887
Seriously. Don't get hurt...

1809
01:10:36,557 --> 01:10:38,456
and stay healthy while running.

1810
01:10:38,457 --> 01:10:39,657
Gosh, you too.

1811
01:10:40,527 --> 01:10:42,366
- Thank you, sir! - Bye.

1812
01:10:42,367 --> 01:10:44,496
- See you! - Have a good day.

1813
01:10:44,497 --> 01:10:45,527
You too!

1814
01:10:46,767 --> 01:10:48,737
(This way.)

1815
01:10:49,337 --> 01:10:50,636
(Sending off the taxi driver)

1816
01:10:50,637 --> 01:10:52,636
- She's so cute! - She even did the gesture.

1817
01:10:52,637 --> 01:10:54,253
Gosh, I've never seen something like that.

1818
01:10:54,277 --> 01:10:56,306
- It's a bit like a sitcom. - You're right.

1819
01:10:56,307 --> 01:10:59,377
In sitcoms, her skirt would be caught in the taxi door.

1820
01:10:59,477 --> 01:11:01,247
Right.

1821
01:11:02,847 --> 01:11:06,216
I've never imagined seeing someone being respectful that way.

1822
01:11:06,217 --> 01:11:08,863
- I've never seen such energy level. - I should learn that from her.

1823
01:11:08,887 --> 01:11:10,356
All right.

1824
01:11:10,357 --> 01:11:11,886
(Setting out with high energy)

1825
01:11:11,887 --> 01:11:12,997
Where is that?

1826
01:11:14,197 --> 01:11:15,627
(Rushing)

1827
01:11:17,227 --> 01:11:19,966
(Her pace gets more and more hurried.)

1828
01:11:19,967 --> 01:11:21,097
All right.

1829
01:11:21,837 --> 01:11:22,906
- Where is that? - What?

1830
01:11:22,907 --> 01:11:25,027
- She's going to paint. - You went to learn to paint?

1831
01:11:25,137 --> 01:11:26,776
Oh, meditation. She must be meditating.

1832
01:11:26,777 --> 01:11:27,877
Meditation?

1833
01:11:28,907 --> 01:11:30,647
(Being careful)

1834
01:11:31,947 --> 01:11:33,716
- Hello. - Hello.

1835
01:11:33,717 --> 01:11:35,317
(She quietly enters a mysterious place.)

1836
01:11:36,087 --> 01:11:38,987
- It's an atelier. - That's right! It's an atelier.

1837
01:11:39,617 --> 01:11:42,457
- You're learning to paint. - Yes, that's my hobby.

1838
01:11:43,587 --> 01:11:45,186
- All right. - All right.

1839
01:11:45,187 --> 01:11:46,997
(Plopping down in her seat in a familiar way)

1840
01:11:48,957 --> 01:11:52,027
It's my fourth time, including today.

1841
01:11:52,597 --> 01:11:56,937
I learn how to sketch with a pencil and coloring it.

1842
01:11:57,007 --> 01:12:00,006
I've been learning it step by step each day.

1843
01:12:00,007 --> 01:12:01,136
She did quite well.

1844
01:12:01,137 --> 01:12:02,647
She really did well.

1845
01:12:02,777 --> 01:12:05,546
It hasn't been long since I moved,

1846
01:12:05,547 --> 01:12:07,676
so I wanted to decorate my bedroom.

1847
01:12:07,677 --> 01:12:10,446
The wallpaper is white, and there's nothing on it.

1848
01:12:10,447 --> 01:12:11,516
Oh, she's right.

1849
01:12:11,517 --> 01:12:15,456
I wanted to decorate the wall with the pictures I painted.

1850
01:12:15,457 --> 01:12:17,387
That's why I went to an atelier.

1851
01:12:20,097 --> 01:12:21,397
(Glancing)

1852
01:12:21,997 --> 01:12:23,727
(Dabbing)

1853
01:12:24,527 --> 01:12:27,237
(Taking a look at the example)

1854
01:12:31,107 --> 01:12:35,577
(She paints the flower without hesitation.)

1855
01:12:37,877 --> 01:12:40,877
(Quietly coloring without saying a word)

1856
01:12:42,277 --> 01:12:45,547
(She paints a picture she'll put in her bedroom.)

1857
01:12:47,557 --> 01:12:50,387
(Soley focusing on the painting)

1858
01:12:52,627 --> 01:12:54,157
(Clattering)

1859
01:12:55,127 --> 01:12:57,567
(At home, her behavior was unpredictable.)

1860
01:12:58,467 --> 01:13:01,567
Actually, I talk to myself a lot...

1861
01:13:01,897 --> 01:13:03,467
when I'm not focusing on anything.

1862
01:13:03,737 --> 01:13:05,666
At least when I paint,

1863
01:13:05,667 --> 01:13:08,777
I can focus on it fully without any thoughts.

1864
01:13:09,607 --> 01:13:12,776
I'd think, "I didn't know I could be this happy."

1865
01:13:12,777 --> 01:13:15,146
It's a place where I can relax.

1866
01:13:15,147 --> 01:13:16,386
- Right. - That's important.

1867
01:13:16,387 --> 01:13:17,446
Yes.

1868
01:13:17,447 --> 01:13:19,356
(Drawing is like meditating.)

1869
01:13:19,357 --> 01:13:20,957
It's really good for your mental health.

1870
01:13:21,087 --> 01:13:22,117
That's right.

1871
01:13:22,587 --> 01:13:24,027
It's important to know how to focus.

1872
01:13:29,467 --> 01:13:30,497
She's immersed in it.

1873
01:13:31,627 --> 01:13:32,767
I'm super focused.

1874
01:13:35,567 --> 01:13:39,707
(It's as if only the picture and Tsuki existed in this world.)

1875
01:13:42,577 --> 01:13:45,406
(She keeps painting, forgetting to talk to herself.)

1876
01:13:45,407 --> 01:13:46,677
She's totally focused.

1877
01:13:49,447 --> 01:13:51,846
- That's right. - Mix it with green.

1878
01:13:51,847 --> 01:13:54,217
- With green? - Yes, and then...

1879
01:13:56,057 --> 01:13:57,287
- All right. - Okay.

1880
01:13:59,157 --> 01:14:00,856
- Amazing. - Are you done?

1881
01:14:00,857 --> 01:14:01,896
What do you think?

1882
01:14:01,897 --> 01:14:03,627
- Oh, my gosh. - What do you think?

1883
01:14:03,897 --> 01:14:05,726
That's the darkest one,

1884
01:14:05,727 --> 01:14:07,537
- and that's the bright one. - That's right.

1885
01:14:07,837 --> 01:14:09,806
- I colored everything. - You drew well.

1886
01:14:09,807 --> 01:14:11,036
- You're good. - You did a good job.

1887
01:14:11,037 --> 01:14:13,107
- Thank you! Yes! - It's pretty.

1888
01:14:13,707 --> 01:14:14,737
It's pretty.

1889
01:14:15,107 --> 01:14:17,177
(Moved)

1890
01:14:18,347 --> 01:14:20,016
- It's finally done. - Great job.

1891
01:14:20,017 --> 01:14:21,346
- I'm so happy. - Yes.

1892
01:14:21,347 --> 01:14:23,347
- Next time, I should paint that. - Yes.

1893
01:14:23,647 --> 01:14:24,717
Great.

1894
01:14:25,187 --> 01:14:27,357
- Have a safe trip home. - Thank you. Bye.

1895
01:14:30,927 --> 01:14:32,027
Hello.

1896
01:14:32,797 --> 01:14:34,226
(Showing)

1897
01:14:34,227 --> 01:14:35,757
- Good. - Thank you.

1898
01:14:36,927 --> 01:14:38,847
What? It looked like you were showing it around.

1899
01:14:39,437 --> 01:14:41,297
That's right. I showed it to him.

1900
01:14:41,537 --> 01:14:43,297
Are you selling it while carrying it around?

1901
01:14:43,937 --> 01:14:46,307
I wanted to show it off a bit.

1902
01:14:46,437 --> 01:14:47,576
I was so happy.

1903
01:14:47,577 --> 01:14:48,837
How should I react to that?

1904
01:14:49,307 --> 01:14:50,707
- "Gosh." - "Gosh."

1905
01:14:52,577 --> 01:14:55,477
(A painting that steals a passerby's attention)

1906
01:14:56,687 --> 01:14:58,917
She's so cute, though.

1907
01:15:00,957 --> 01:15:02,556
You should carry it carefully. It'd stain your coat.

1908
01:15:02,557 --> 01:15:03,727
That's right.

1909
01:15:04,287 --> 01:15:05,356
It did get on my coat.

1910
01:15:05,357 --> 01:15:07,496
(It did get on my coat.)

1911
01:15:07,497 --> 01:15:08,557
I see.

1912
01:15:08,697 --> 01:15:10,697
That was the last day of that coat.

1913
01:15:10,767 --> 01:15:12,096
- Goodness. - Goodness.

1914
01:15:12,097 --> 01:15:13,937
- That's one expensive painting. - Bye.

1915
01:15:14,267 --> 01:15:16,237
It's here.

1916
01:15:16,667 --> 01:15:19,737
(Striding somewhere while carrying an expensive painting)

1917
01:15:20,737 --> 01:15:21,776
(Going straight to the store she sees)

1918
01:15:21,777 --> 01:15:22,906
Where are you going again...

1919
01:15:22,907 --> 01:15:24,787
- Where is the entrance? - while carrying that?

1920
01:15:25,607 --> 01:15:27,477
(Cautious)

1921
01:15:29,617 --> 01:15:31,046
(Suddenly displaying her painting in front of someone's store)

1922
01:15:31,047 --> 01:15:32,986
- It suits the store. - Right?

1923
01:15:32,987 --> 01:15:34,516
- You're right. - That's what I thought too.

1924
01:15:34,517 --> 01:15:35,956
That's why I took a photo of it.

1925
01:15:35,957 --> 01:15:37,256
- Nice. - Right?

1926
01:15:37,257 --> 01:15:38,787
(Happy)

1927
01:15:40,857 --> 01:15:42,696
Hello!

1928
01:15:42,697 --> 01:15:44,566
- Where is it? - Hello.

1929
01:15:44,567 --> 01:15:45,667
A cooking class?

1930
01:15:46,637 --> 01:15:47,666
(She dropped by a nearby store since she went to the atelier.)

1931
01:15:47,667 --> 01:15:48,836
Is it a Japanese curry restaurant?

1932
01:15:48,837 --> 01:15:50,406
I came by because they said...

1933
01:15:50,407 --> 01:15:51,966
- I could get natto here. - Is that so?

1934
01:15:51,967 --> 01:15:53,537
There's a place that sells natto?

1935
01:15:53,707 --> 01:15:56,306
It's a restaurant specializing in natto.

1936
01:15:56,307 --> 01:15:57,606
There's a restaurant that specializes in natto?

1937
01:15:57,607 --> 01:15:59,327
(She's at a natto-specializing restaurant.)

1938
01:15:59,717 --> 01:16:02,076
I'll take one large box.

1939
01:16:02,077 --> 01:16:03,717
- One large box? Got it. - Yes.

1940
01:16:03,847 --> 01:16:04,946
Thank you.

1941
01:16:04,947 --> 01:16:06,757
(Buying big-sized natto shyly)

1942
01:16:07,287 --> 01:16:08,387
I'm so excited.

1943
01:16:09,157 --> 01:16:10,827
- I'll help you with the payment. - Sure.

1944
01:16:11,327 --> 01:16:13,526
I saw natto gelato too.

1945
01:16:13,527 --> 01:16:14,726
We do have natto gelato.

1946
01:16:14,727 --> 01:16:16,527
- You do? - Do you want it?

1947
01:16:17,197 --> 01:16:18,267
Yes.

1948
01:16:19,227 --> 01:16:20,636
- Yes, I'll take one. - All right.

1949
01:16:20,637 --> 01:16:22,036
Amazing!

1950
01:16:22,037 --> 01:16:24,206
- Thank you. - Natto gelato?

1951
01:16:24,207 --> 01:16:25,367
I can't imagine its taste.

1952
01:16:26,877 --> 01:16:29,376
I really wanted to try it.

1953
01:16:29,377 --> 01:16:31,283
(There are natto beans here and there in the gelato.)

1954
01:16:31,307 --> 01:16:32,977
(Drooling)

1955
01:16:33,177 --> 01:16:35,446
- I'll give you the gelato first. - Thank you.

1956
01:16:35,447 --> 01:16:36,647
Thank you for this.

1957
01:16:37,517 --> 01:16:40,787
(Feeling uplifted by natto gelato she's been wanting to have)

1958
01:16:41,617 --> 01:16:44,527
Oh, gosh. It's my first time trying it.

1959
01:16:45,457 --> 01:16:46,657
(Exclaiming)

1960
01:16:49,827 --> 01:16:52,527
(Taking a bite with high expectations)

1961
01:16:54,137 --> 01:16:55,297
It must be good.

1962
01:16:56,437 --> 01:16:57,437
Is it good?

1963
01:16:58,607 --> 01:17:00,706
It's good. It's really good.

1964
01:17:00,707 --> 01:17:01,907
It was so good.

1965
01:17:02,007 --> 01:17:04,147
I went to paint earlier.

1966
01:17:04,277 --> 01:17:06,246
- I carried the painting here. - He didn't ask.

1967
01:17:06,247 --> 01:17:08,316
Stop! Stop it, Tsuki!

1968
01:17:08,317 --> 01:17:12,716
I thought it'd be too much for you if I carried it inside.

1969
01:17:12,717 --> 01:17:13,786
You can show it to me.

1970
01:17:13,787 --> 01:17:15,516
Really? I'll bring it. One second.

1971
01:17:15,517 --> 01:17:17,957
- Ta-da. - It's amazing.

1972
01:17:18,027 --> 01:17:20,827
I should eat while looking at it.

1973
01:17:20,927 --> 01:17:23,267
- She's really sociable. - Nice.

1974
01:17:23,767 --> 01:17:26,997
She gets along with others really well.

1975
01:17:28,667 --> 01:17:29,766
(Super sociable Tsuki has a good time with him...)

1976
01:17:29,767 --> 01:17:30,836
(even though it's her first time seeing him)

1977
01:17:30,837 --> 01:17:32,807
She gets close with others really fast.

1978
01:17:33,277 --> 01:17:34,307
I know.

1979
01:17:35,337 --> 01:17:37,706
That was good. I finished it.

1980
01:17:37,707 --> 01:17:39,306
- I'll throw it for you. - Thank you.

1981
01:17:39,307 --> 01:17:41,146
(Thank you for the gelato.)

1982
01:17:41,147 --> 01:17:42,417
Yes!

1983
01:17:42,547 --> 01:17:43,947
I'm so happy.

1984
01:17:44,687 --> 01:17:46,116
How cute. Thank you.

1985
01:17:46,117 --> 01:17:48,216
- There's an instruction inside. - Okay.

1986
01:17:48,217 --> 01:17:49,487
All right.

1987
01:17:49,887 --> 01:17:51,956
Thank you! Bye!

1988
01:17:51,957 --> 01:17:53,957
(She leaves the restaurant feeling more excited.)

1989
01:17:54,197 --> 01:17:57,227
I'll come again. Thank you.

1990
01:17:58,227 --> 01:17:59,367
That was good.

1991
01:18:00,297 --> 01:18:02,336
- The painting you drew in one hand. - That's right!

1992
01:18:02,337 --> 01:18:04,177
- And natto in the other hand. - That's right.

1993
01:18:04,867 --> 01:18:05,966
You have the whole world...

1994
01:18:05,967 --> 01:18:07,247
- in your hands. - That's right.

1995
01:18:08,077 --> 01:18:09,807
Shall I go back now?

1996
01:18:11,507 --> 01:18:13,777
I'm back.

1997
01:18:15,217 --> 01:18:18,016
(She gets changed into something comfy as soon as she gets home.)

1998
01:18:18,017 --> 01:18:19,517
(Moving)

1999
01:18:20,157 --> 01:18:21,756
(Hurrying to the bedroom with her painting)

2000
01:18:21,757 --> 01:18:23,887
It might be too high to hang.

2001
01:18:24,987 --> 01:18:27,226
- It suits her room really well. - It suits her room.

2002
01:18:27,227 --> 01:18:28,657
Right?

2003
01:18:28,727 --> 01:18:30,726
She must've contemplated a lot to choose that...

2004
01:18:30,727 --> 01:18:32,627
before she painted it.

2005
01:18:33,197 --> 01:18:37,106
Should I put it here and put another one next to it later?

2006
01:18:37,107 --> 01:18:38,266
(Thinking)

2007
01:18:38,267 --> 01:18:39,637
It'll be pretty nice.

2008
01:18:40,577 --> 01:18:42,507
- Like this. - It suits the room.

2009
01:18:43,307 --> 01:18:44,347
Okay, over here.

2010
01:18:45,577 --> 01:18:47,147
(Struggling)

2011
01:18:47,547 --> 01:18:49,587
(Driving the pin into the wall with her whole body)

2012
01:18:49,987 --> 01:18:51,887
She's not hanging it in the middle?

2013
01:18:52,087 --> 01:18:55,257
I want to hang the picture I'm painting next to it.

2014
01:18:55,487 --> 01:18:56,557
Yes.

2015
01:18:57,257 --> 01:18:58,627
Oh, my!

2016
01:19:00,927 --> 01:19:02,026
It's so pretty.

2017
01:19:02,027 --> 01:19:04,567
(It's so pretty.)

2018
01:19:04,667 --> 01:19:06,067
It's pretty.

2019
01:19:06,127 --> 01:19:07,897
- Yes, continue painting. - I will.

2020
01:19:08,367 --> 01:19:10,537
Even though I painted it myself,

2021
01:19:12,067 --> 01:19:14,136
I thought, "Who painted this? It's so pretty."

2022
01:19:14,137 --> 01:19:15,906
That's how well the picture turned out.

2023
01:19:15,907 --> 01:19:17,677
I kept thinking that.

2024
01:19:18,677 --> 01:19:19,716
It's so pretty.

2025
01:19:19,717 --> 01:19:23,387
I wondered where to place my next painting.

2026
01:19:24,787 --> 01:19:25,886
It's satisfying.

2027
01:19:25,887 --> 01:19:27,257
That's the fun of life.

2028
01:19:28,217 --> 01:19:29,757
And now...

2029
01:19:30,457 --> 01:19:32,627
First, let's turn on the lights.

2030
01:19:34,057 --> 01:19:35,857
Okay. Let's get started.

2031
01:19:36,627 --> 01:19:39,297
(Let's see.)

2032
01:19:40,567 --> 01:19:41,666
- She's eating again? - She's cooking again?

2033
01:19:41,667 --> 01:19:42,736
(It's already time for dinner.)

2034
01:19:42,737 --> 01:19:44,106
- This time... - Seriously.

2035
01:19:44,107 --> 01:19:45,166
Let's ruin it again.

2036
01:19:45,167 --> 01:19:46,167
(Joking)

2037
01:19:46,168 --> 01:19:47,307
No!

2038
01:19:47,677 --> 01:19:49,436
I was confident with this one.

2039
01:19:49,437 --> 01:19:51,947
Cream cheese, natto,

2040
01:19:52,377 --> 01:19:53,717
and pollack roe.

2041
01:19:54,577 --> 01:19:57,717
(Cutting the cream cheese first)

2042
01:19:58,487 --> 01:20:00,516
(What's the next ingredient?)

2043
01:20:00,517 --> 01:20:02,317
(Spicy and salty salted pollack roe)

2044
01:20:03,357 --> 01:20:05,827
(She rolls the perilla leaves and cuts them thinly.)

2045
01:20:06,297 --> 01:20:07,297
(Placing one by one)

2046
01:20:07,298 --> 01:20:08,327
What is that?

2047
01:20:08,727 --> 01:20:09,797
Natto.

2048
01:20:10,367 --> 01:20:11,497
Oh, my.

2049
01:20:14,767 --> 01:20:16,667
- Oh, my gosh. So cute. - It looks delicious.

2050
01:20:17,867 --> 01:20:20,807
(Yes, this is it!)

2051
01:20:23,447 --> 01:20:24,447
Soy sauce?

2052
01:20:24,448 --> 01:20:26,477
That's tsuyu.

2053
01:20:27,517 --> 01:20:31,317
And the leftover braised kimchi.

2054
01:20:33,587 --> 01:20:34,686
- Nice. - It looks like a dish...

2055
01:20:34,687 --> 01:20:35,786
- Great. - from a popular restaurant.

2056
01:20:35,787 --> 01:20:37,356
It became better after boiling for a long time.

2057
01:20:37,357 --> 01:20:39,157
These are done.

2058
01:20:40,297 --> 01:20:41,557
I had to take a photo.

2059
01:20:42,327 --> 01:20:43,497
Will it last two weeks?

2060
01:20:44,267 --> 01:20:45,466
Forget about that.

2061
01:20:45,467 --> 01:20:46,866
You won't even remember that you took a photo of it.

2062
01:20:46,867 --> 01:20:48,466
Right. I need to find it.

2063
01:20:48,467 --> 01:20:50,436
All right.

2064
01:20:50,437 --> 01:20:54,377
(Tsuki's simple dinner)

2065
01:20:55,777 --> 01:20:57,547
(Heave-ho)

2066
01:20:58,107 --> 01:21:00,777
(It looks delicious.)

2067
01:21:01,117 --> 01:21:03,987
Dad is working.

2068
01:21:04,447 --> 01:21:05,747
There's a family group chat.

2069
01:21:06,817 --> 01:21:08,417
You don't have one?

2070
01:21:08,617 --> 01:21:09,627
No.

2071
01:21:12,027 --> 01:21:14,526
- Hi, honey. - Hi.

2072
01:21:14,527 --> 01:21:16,497
(Video chatting with her mother)

2073
01:21:17,367 --> 01:21:19,326
- That's my mom. - She's really young.

2074
01:21:19,327 --> 01:21:21,597
- She's young. - Your mother is really young.

2075
01:21:21,837 --> 01:21:23,036
- How old... - How old is she?

2076
01:21:23,037 --> 01:21:25,467
I think she's 53 or 54.

2077
01:21:25,637 --> 01:21:26,836
What about your dad?

2078
01:21:26,837 --> 01:21:28,736
My dad is even younger. He's 50.

2079
01:21:28,737 --> 01:21:29,906
- Your dad is 50? - Yes.

2080
01:21:29,907 --> 01:21:32,577
That's your big uncle.

2081
01:21:33,747 --> 01:21:35,847
Come pay respects with a bow during the holiday.

2082
01:21:37,787 --> 01:21:39,147
What's Dad up to?

2083
01:21:41,457 --> 01:21:45,787
- Look. I made dinner. - You made dinner?

2084
01:21:46,227 --> 01:21:47,357
